@* A record written out longhand — "7 W · 3 L" — in the app's two outcome colours. It is what a
   figure is shown *beside*: the caption under a win-rate tile, the line under an adversary card's
   name, the run behind a form strip, the header of a scoped statistics page.

   Four of these were spelled out in markup and had already drifted in two ways at once: the pair
   of utilities was written `font-medium text-accent-700` in two places and `text-accent-700
   font-medium` in the other two, and the separator was a bare "·" between two spans in three of
   them and part of a third span in the fourth — so the dot inherited a different tone depending
   on where you read it. The wording lives in one place now, with the separator inside it.

   The figures are the caller's tone-free ones on purpose: jade for what was won and ember for
   what was lost is the same decision WinTone makes about a rate, and the two have to agree. Size
   and any surrounding punctuation are inherited from whatever the tally is set in, since these
   sit in captions from 10px to 14px. *@

<span class="tabular-nums"><span class="font-medium text-accent-700">@Wins W</span> · <span class="font-medium text-red-600">@Losses L</span></span>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public int Wins { get; set; }
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public int Losses { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The same record as a plain string, for the callers that can only take one — a
    /// tile's caption, a tooltip, a record sheet's summary row.</summary>
    public static string Text(int wins, int losses) => $"{wins} W · {losses} L";
}
